### What problem does a Redis distributed lock solve?

When you scale a service horizontally for availability, every instance has the same scheduled job. Without coordination, a cron at 2 AM fires on all five instances and the interest posting runs five times. You need mutual exclusion across machines. A local Java lock cannot provide this because each JVM has its own lock object. Redis, being a single shared fast store, becomes the coordination point.

```java
package com.bank.scheduler;

import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Component
public class InterestPostingJob {

    // Without a distributed lock, all 5 instances run this at 2 AM
    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")
    public void postDailyInterest() {
        // Rajesh's SAV2001 gets interest posted 5 times!
        System.out.println("Posting interest for all accounts...");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (WRONG -- runs on all 5 instances):
Instance-1: Posting interest for all accounts...
Instance-2: Posting interest for all accounts...
Instance-3: Posting interest for all accounts...
Instance-4: Posting interest for all accounts...
Instance-5: Posting interest for all accounts...
```

### How does setIfAbsent with TTL work?

The core operation is Redis SET key value NX PX milliseconds. NX means "only set if the key does not already exist," which is atomic, so exactly one of the racing instances wins. PX sets an expiry in milliseconds. In Spring Data Redis this is `opsForValue().setIfAbsent()` with a Duration, which returns true to the one winner and false to everyone else.

```java
package com.bank.lock;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.UUID;

@Component
public class RedisDistributedLock {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public RedisDistributedLock(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
    }

    public String tryAcquire(String lockKey, Duration ttl) {
        String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        Boolean acquired = redis.opsForValue()
            .setIfAbsent(lockKey, token, ttl); // SET NX PX
        return Boolean.TRUE.equals(acquired) ? token : null;
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Instance-1 calls tryAcquire --> token = "a1b2c3d4" (won)
Instance-2 calls tryAcquire --> token = null (lost)
Instance-3 calls tryAcquire --> token = null (lost)
```

### Why is a TTL mandatory?

If the winner acquires the lock and then its JVM crashes or the machine dies before releasing, without a TTL the lock key stays forever. The job never runs again on any instance -- a permanent deadlock. The TTL is a safety valve: the lock auto-expires, so the next scheduled run can acquire it. Set the TTL comfortably longer than the worst-case job duration so the lock does not expire while the job is still running.

```java
package com.bank.lock;

public class TtlReasoning {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long worstCaseJobMs = 30_000;  // 30 seconds
        long safetyMargin   = 2;       // 2x multiplier
        long ttlMs          = worstCaseJobMs * safetyMargin;

        System.out.println("Worst-case job duration: " + worstCaseJobMs + " ms");
        System.out.println("TTL with safety margin:  " + ttlMs + " ms");
        // TTL = 60 seconds; lock auto-expires even if holder crashes
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Worst-case job duration: 30000 ms
TTL with safety margin:  60000 ms
```

### Why must the lock value be a unique token?

The lock value must be a unique identifier (UUID or instance-id plus timestamp). This prevents the classic wrong-release bug: instance A acquires the lock, the job runs long and the TTL expires, Redis frees the lock, instance B acquires it, and then instance A finishes and blindly deletes the lock -- but it just deleted B's lock. By storing a unique token and releasing only if the stored token equals mine, A sees the token no longer matches and does not delete B's lock.

```java
package com.bank.lock;

public class WrongReleaseBug {
    // WRONG: release without checking the token
    public void releaseUnsafe(String lockKey) {
        // redis.delete(lockKey);
        // Instance A deletes Instance B's lock!
    }

    // CORRECT: release only if the token matches (see Lua script below)
    public void releaseSafe(String lockKey, String myToken) {
        // Lua script checks: if GET(key) == myToken then DEL(key)
        System.out.println("Only delete if stored token equals: " + myToken);
    }
}
```

### Why must release be atomic with a Lua script?

Releasing naively means GET the key, check if it equals my token, then DEL it. But between the GET and the DEL, the TTL could expire and another instance could acquire. A Lua script runs atomically on the Redis server, so the check and delete happen as one indivisible operation.

```java
package com.bank.lock;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.data.redis.core.script.DefaultRedisScript;
import java.util.Collections;

public class AtomicRelease {

    private static final String LUA_RELEASE =
        "if redis.call('get', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then " +
        "  return redis.call('del', KEYS[1]) " +
        "else " +
        "  return 0 " +
        "end";

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public AtomicRelease(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
    }

    public boolean release(String lockKey, String token) {
        DefaultRedisScript<Long> script = new DefaultRedisScript<>(LUA_RELEASE, Long.class);
        Long result = redis.execute(script, Collections.singletonList(lockKey), token);
        return result != null && result == 1L;
        // Returns 1 if deleted (my lock), 0 if token mismatch (not mine)
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Instance A calls release with token "a1b2c3d4" --> 1 (deleted own lock)
Instance B calls release with token "x9y8z7" on A's lock --> 0 (mismatch, not deleted)
```

### How do you put it all together for a scheduled task?

The complete pattern: acquire with setIfAbsent, run the job if acquired, release with Lua in a finally block.

```java
package com.bank.scheduler;

import com.bank.lock.RedisDistributedLock;
import com.bank.lock.AtomicRelease;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.time.Duration;

@Component
public class InterestPostingJobSafe {

    private static final String LOCK_KEY = "lock:interest-posting";
    private static final Duration TTL = Duration.ofSeconds(60);

    private final RedisDistributedLock lock;
    private final AtomicRelease release;

    public InterestPostingJobSafe(RedisDistributedLock lock, AtomicRelease release) {
        this.lock = lock;
        this.release = release;
    }

    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")
    public void postDailyInterest() {
        String token = lock.tryAcquire(LOCK_KEY, TTL);
        if (token == null) {
            System.out.println("Another instance holds the lock. Skipping.");
            return;
        }
        try {
            System.out.println("Lock acquired. Posting interest for SAV2001...");
            // ... actual interest calculation for Rajesh, Priya ...
        } finally {
            release.release(LOCK_KEY, token);
            System.out.println("Lock released.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (Instance 1 wins):
Instance-1: Lock acquired. Posting interest for SAV2001...
Instance-1: Lock released.
Instance-2: Another instance holds the lock. Skipping.
Instance-3: Another instance holds the lock. Skipping.
```

### How do you handle long-running jobs with a watchdog?

If a job might run longer than the TTL, a background watchdog periodically extends the TTL via a token-checked Lua script that calls PEXPIRE only if the token matches. This is what Redisson does automatically with lock leases.

```java
package com.bank.lock;

import java.util.concurrent.ScheduledExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class LockWatchdog {

    private static final String LUA_EXTEND =
        "if redis.call('get', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then " +
        "  return redis.call('pexpire', KEYS[1], ARGV[2]) " +
        "else return 0 end";

    private final ScheduledExecutorService scheduler =
        Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor();

    public void startWatchdog(String lockKey, String token, long ttlMs) {
        long renewInterval = ttlMs / 3; // renew at 1/3 of TTL
        scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> {
            // Extend TTL only if we still own the lock
            System.out.println("Watchdog: extending TTL for " + lockKey);
        }, renewInterval, renewInterval, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
    }

    public void stopWatchdog() {
        scheduler.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most frequent mistakes involve missing TTL, missing token check, and not making the job idempotent.

```java
package com.bank.lock;

public class CommonMistakes {
    // WRONG 1: no TTL -- holder crash = permanent deadlock
    // redis.opsForValue().setIfAbsent("lock:interest", "locked");

    // CORRECT 1: always set TTL
    // redis.opsForValue().setIfAbsent("lock:interest", token, Duration.ofSeconds(60));

    // WRONG 2: constant lock value -- anyone can release
    // redis.opsForValue().setIfAbsent("lock:interest", "LOCKED", ttl);
    // redis.delete("lock:interest"); // any instance deletes it

    // CORRECT 2: unique token + Lua release

    // WRONG 3: job is not idempotent -- rare double-run doubles interest
    // CORRECT 3: check posting_date marker so double-run is harmless
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: no TTL, constant value, non-idempotent job");
        System.out.println("CORRECT: TTL + UUID token + Lua release + idempotent job");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: no TTL, constant value, non-idempotent job
CORRECT: TTL + UUID token + Lua release + idempotent job
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A single-Redis lock is not a perfect distributed lock under network partitions and Redis failover. It is a practical "mostly once" guard. For stronger guarantees, use Redisson, which implements the Redlock algorithm, lock leasing, watchdog renewal, and reentrancy. For money-critical jobs, always combine the lock with an idempotent job so that even a rare double-run does not double-post.

- The lock makes double execution rare; idempotency makes it harmless.
- Redisson's watchdog automatically extends the lock while the job runs.
- For critical financial operations, add a posting-date marker so the job is truly idempotent.
- Be honest about guarantees: a single-Redis lock is practical, not perfect.

```java
package com.bank.scheduler;

import java.time.LocalDate;

public class IdempotentInterestPosting {
